    "description": "A carrier hook proves that the managed request or carrier method executed in the attached process; the optional VoicePlayer external-source preparation hook records the formatted externalSourceKey and its Event/object/handle arguments immediately before the external-post helper; an AudioAdapter hook proves that an integer Event request reached the adapter. Neither alone proves Wwise acceptance, selected media, or audibility.",
    "eventIdJoin": "eventHash against the current language Audio index is a static name/media candidate join, not an observed Wwise selection.",
    "nativeTrace": "Native observations are accepted only from the hash-locked AkSoundEngine.dll. External-source descriptors, the source-manager key join, key-to-decoder registry, and Wwise open/read calls are execution evidence, but a capture must still show their correlation before claiming a key-to-file mapping. The agent resolves the exact source-manager hash-table node by serial at constructor return and join/lookup entry, records the constructor status (1 for the successful registration branch and 0x34 for this build's allocation-failure branch), and the importer reports pointer intersections as same-entry evidence. Native events also carry the same-thread managed hook stack when a synchronous bridge is active; the importer reports exact path matches at that boundary without upgrading later asynchronous events. Each native call also carries an exact same-thread nativeParentCaptureId when another attached native hook is synchronously active; nativePairing.nativeCallRelations reports only those resolved parent/child hook pairs. The importer separately compares native resolver/queue descriptor context pointers with the managed external callback cookie; this is callback-mapping evidence, not the Wwise external-source cookie or a file/PCM join. The optional VoicePlayer helper hook records its fifth ABI argument as stackArguments.codec at entry stack +0x28, so managed codec values can be compared with copied descriptor +0x04 without assuming a numeric-domain conversion. Provider preparation records decoder +0x58 only after the helper returns, and the importer compares it separately with device/async-read provider pointers as a bounded same-object check. The source-state initializer at +0xd1f90 samples the source-state object, config +0x34 key, incoming sourceInfo, and post-write +0x268/+0x288 fields; pointer/key intersections with manager joins, provider owners, decoder owners, and sourceInfo pointers are initialization continuity only. The sourceInfo selector samples candidate descriptor +0x10, while the source-info consumer and provider-preparation owner sample the copied source +0x338; pointer intersections are reported as bounded selector-to-owner continuity, not as a path or file identity. The default-open stack argument-5 provider context is sampled after the call, including its +0x10 handle; async-read descriptors sample their provider object's +0x10 handle, and an exact handle intersection is reported as open-to-read continuity. The generic decoder entry at +0x1c7ec0 is also sampled with its decoder owner +0x268 key, provider +0x58 interface, float-output slot, frame-count slot, and native return address; return address +0x1801c4780 identifies the static PCM16-producing caller while +0x1801c49c1/+0x1801c4a43 identify refill/retry callers. Registry/provider intersections prove bounded continuity into an actual decode call when observed, while the statically known caller still owns float-to-PCM16 conversion. Provider-array dispatch, ReadFileEx descriptor fields, completion request fields, and codec callback pointers are captured as bounded ABI evidence; none alone proves a key-to-file join, decoded PCM, or audibility."
